          "derivation": "New Persian ناو nāv continues Middle Persian nāw 'ship' < Old Persian nāv- (𐎴𐎠𐎺) < Proto-Iranian *náHuš < PIE *néh₂us; the long ā and the -v are simply preserved, so the word is one of the few Persian nouns whose shape is unchanged from the Achaemenid period. Its double semantics — 'ship' and 'trough / channel / gutter' — is old and internal: a dugout boat and a hollowed log gutter are the same object (compare Welsh noe 'kneading trough', Norwegian no 'trough from a hollowed trunk', OHG nuosc 'trough, gully', all under Pokorny's naus-1), so no borrowing or contamination need be assumed. Borhan-e Qate' gives exactly Nourai's range: 'کشتی و جهاز کوچک — و ناودان بام خانه — و آنچه گندم از دول به گلوی آسیا ریزد — و چوب میان‌خالی کرده که … آب از آن به چرخ آسیا خورد' (with the Attar verse Nourai prints). ناودان nāv-dān is a New Persian compound of nāv + dān, the second element being the old Iranian *dānu- 'river, flowing water' (Av. dānu-, whence the Danube/Don names — Nourai's ☞Dâ 2), hence literally 'water-channel'; already Horn p.229 cites 'nāv \"Wasserkanal; Schiffchen\", nāvdān'. Nourai's extra list is all regular Persian word-formation on nāv: ناوشکن 'destroyer' (nāv-šekan 'ship-breaker'), ناودیس 'syncline' (nāv-dīs 'boat-shaped'), ناوسار, ناخدا (< nāv-xodā 'ship-master'), ناوگان 'fleet', ناوبان, ناودار — the military ones being 20th-century coinages of the Farhangestān, as Mo'in marks them '(فره.)'.",
